ial design theory, where the maximum number of edge-disjoint triangles in \\( K_8 \\) is 8.\n\n4. **Construction**:\n   - Dividing the princes into two groups of four and forming all possible triples within each group results in 8 triples (4 from each group), ensuring no pair is repeated.\n   - This method uses all pairs within each group, leaving cross-group pairs unused but valid under the problem's constraints.\n\nThus, the maximum number of nights the king can send three princes without any two repeating a pair is \\(\\boxed{8}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nWe need to determine the maximum number of nights that the king can send three of his sons to guard the golden apples, ensuring that no two sons guard together more than once. We have 8 sons in total.\n\n1. **Estimating the Maximum Number of Guard Nights**:\n    - Each son can guard with at most 2 other distinct sons in one night.\n    - In total, a son can only guard with 7 other sons.\n    - For each son, after three nights of guarding, he will have guarded with 6 different sons.\n    - After three guards, there will be one son whom he hasn't guarded with yet.\n\n2. **Pairs Concept**:\n    - If we consider any son, say Son A, he will not be able to guard with one specific son after his three nights. This pairs him in a unique avoidance with another son, say Son B, in a symmetric situation.\n    - Thus, there exists a minimum of 4 pairs (A with B, C with D, etc.) where the paired sons won't have guarded together.\n\n3. **Total Number of Pairs**:\n    - The total number of possible pairs is \\(\\binom{8}{2} = 28\\) pairs.\n    - We know that some pairs cannot be used. Each exclusion of guarding reduces the possible guard pairs effectively.\n    - Since there are 4 such pairs due to the symmetric avoidance:\n      \\[\n      \\text{Total usable pairs} = 28 - 4 = 24\n      \\]\n\n4. **Calculating Maximum Number of Nights**:\n    - Each night involves 3 sons. This forms 3 unique pairs per night from the 8 so