 parabola \\( y^2 = 2px \\) can be parametrized as \\( \\left( \\frac{p}{2}t^2, pt \\right) \\), where \\( t \\) is a parameter. The vertex \\( O \\) is at \\( (0, 0) \\), and the focus \\( F \\) is at \\( \\left( \\frac{p}{2}, 0 \\right) \\).\n\n2. **Chords Through the Focus**  \n   Let \\( AB \\) and \\( CD \\) be chords passing through \\( F \\). For points \\( A \\) and \\( B \\) with parameters \\( t_1 \\) and \\( t_2 \\), the chord \\( AB \\) passes through \\( F \\). Using properties of the parabola, the product of parameters for such chords satisfies \\( t_1 t_2 = -1 \\). Similarly, for chord \\( CD \\), \\( t_3 t_4 = -1 \\).\n\n3. **Equations of Circles**  \n   - **Circle 1** (diameter \\( AB \\)): Midpoint \\( M_1 = \\left( \\frac{p}{4}(t_1^2 + t_2^2), \\frac{p}{2}(t_1 + t_2) \\right) \\).  \n   - **Circle 2** (diameter \\( CD \\)): Midpoint \\( M_2 = \\left( \\frac{p}{4}(t_3^2 + t_4^2), \\frac{p}{2}(t_3 + t_4) \\right) \\).  \n   Using the diameter endpoints, the equations of the circles are derived. Both circles share the constant term \\( -\\frac{3p^2}{4} \\) due to \\( t_1 t_2 = t_3 t_4 = -1 \\).\n\n4. **Common Chord Equation**  \n   Subtracting the equations of the two circles eliminates the quadratic terms, leaving the linear equation of their common chord:  \n   \\[\n   (t_1^2 + t_2^2 - t_3^2 - t_4^2)x + 2(t_1 + t_2 - t_3 - t_4)y = 0.\n   \\]  \n   Substituting \\( (0, 0) \\) into this equation yields \\( 0 = 0 \\), confirming that the origin \\( O \\) lies on the common chord.\n\n5. **Power of a Point**  \n   The power of \\( O \\) with respect to both circles is computed and shown to be equal (\\( -\\frac{3p^2}{4} \\)). This implies \\( O \\) lies on the radical axis (common chord) of the two circles.\n\n---\n\n### **Conclusion**  \nThe common chord of the two circles passes through the vertex \\( O \\) because:  \n1. Substituting \\( (0, 0) \\) satisfies the common chord equation.  \n2. The power of \\( O \\) with respect to both circles is equal, placing \\( O \\) on their radical axis.  \n\nThus, the common chord **must pass through the vertex \\( O \\)**.\n\n**Final Answer**  \n\\boxed{\\text{The common chord passes through the vertex } O.}", "ground_truth_solution": ": \n\nTo show that the common chord of two circles each defined by chords passing through the focus $F$ of the parabola \\( y^2 = 2px \\) passes through the vertex \\( O \\), we can proceed as follows:\n\n1. **Define the Chords**:\n    Let \\( A(x_A, y_A) \\) and \\( B(x_B, y_B) \\) be points on the parabola such that the line segment \\( AB \\) is a chord passing through the focus \\( F \\). Similarly, let \\( C(x_C, y_C) \\) and \\( D(x_D, y_D) \\) be other points on the parabola such that the line segment \\( CD \\) is another chord passing through the focus \\( F \\).\n\n2. **Equation of Circle**:\n    The chords \\( AB \\) and \\( CD \\) define two circles. The circle defined by \\( AB \\) has the equation:\n    \\[\n    \\left(x - x_A\\right)\\left(x - x_B\\right) + \\left(y - y_A\\right)\\left(y - y_B\\right) = 0\n    \\]\n    Similarly, the circle defined by \\( CD \\) has the equation:\n    \\[\n    \\left(x - x_C\\right)\\left(x - x_D\\right) + \\left(y - y_C\\right)\\left(y - y_D\\right) = 0\n    \\]\n\n3. **Subtract the Equations**:\n    Subtracting the two equations will help in finding the equation of the common chord:\n    \\[\n    \\left[\\left(x - x_A\\right)\\left(x - x_B\\right) + \\left(y - y_A\\right)\\left(y - y_B\\right)\\right] - \\left[\\left(x - x_C\\right)\\left(x - x_D\\right) + \\left(y - y_C\\right)\\left(y - y_D\\right)\\right] = 0,\n    \\]\n    which can be rewritten as:\n    \\[\n    \\left(x_A - x_C \\right) x + \\left(x_B - x_D \\right) x + \\left(y_A - y_C \\right) y + \\left(y_B - y_D \\right) y + \\left(x_A x_B + y_A y_B\\right) - \\left(x_C x_D + y_C y_D\\right) = 0\n    \\]\n\n4. **Simplify the Common Chord Equation**:\n    Simplified, this becomes:\n    \\[ \n    ax + by + k = 0,\n    \\]\n    where