 b c d by AM-GM.\n\nLet's prove that. Apply AM-GM to each pair:\n\nFor each pair (a + b) >= 2\u221a(ab)\n\nSimilarly, (b + c) >= 2\u221a(bc)\n\n(c + d) >= 2\u221a(cd)\n\n(d + a) >= 2\u221a(da)\n\nMultiply all four inequalities:\n\n(a + b)(b + c)(c + d)(d + a) >= (2\u221a(ab))(2\u221a(bc))(2\u221a(cd))(2\u221a(da)) =16 * \u221a(ab)\u221a(bc)\u221a(cd)\u221a(da)\n\nSimplify the product under the square roots:\n\n\u221a(ab * bc * cd * da) = \u221a(a^2 b^2 c^2 d^2) = abcd\n\nTherefore,\n\n(a + b)(b + c)(c + d)(d + a) >=16 abcd\n\nWhich gives ( (a + b)(b + c)(c + d)(d + a) ) / (a b c d ) >=16\n\nThus, from the earlier AM-GM step, A >=4*16=64\n\nEquality holds when all the inequalities used become equalities, i.e., when a = b, b = c, c = d, d = a. Which implies a = b = c = d.\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of A is 64, achieved when a = b = c = d.\n\nHence, after thorough analysis using various methods\u2014testing symmetric cases, applying AM-GM inequality, checking critical points via Lagrange multipliers, and verifying with multiple test cases\u2014the conclusion is that the minimum value of the expression A is 64, achieved when all variables are equal.", "deepseek_solution": "To find the minimum value of the expression\n\n$$\nA = \\left( \\frac{a+b}{c} \\right)^4 + \\left( \\frac{b+c}{d} \\right)^4 + \\left( \\frac{c+d}{a} \\right)^4 + \\left( \\frac{d+a}{b} \\right)^4\n$$\n\nfor positive numbers \\(a, b, c, d\\), we proceed as follows:\n\n### Step 1: Symmetry Consideration\nAssume \\(a = b = c = d\\). Substituting these values:\n- Each term becomes \\(\\left(\\frac{2a}{a}\\right)^4 = 2^4 = 16\\).\n- Thus, \\(A = 4 \\times 16 = 64\\).\n\n### Step 2: Applying the AM-GM Inequality\nThe Arithmetic Mean-Geometric Mean (AM-GM) inequality states that for non-negative real numbers, the arithmetic mean is at least the geometric mean. Applying AM-GM to each pair:\n- \\(a + b \\geq 2\\sqrt{ab}\\), \\(b + c \\geq 2\\sqrt{bc}\\), \\(c + d \\geq 2\\sqrt{cd}\\), \\(d + a \\geq 2\\sqrt{da}\\).\n\nMultiplying these inequalities:\n$$\n(a + b)(b + c)(c + d)(d + a) \\geq 16 \\sqrt{ab} \\cdot \\sqrt{bc} \\cdot \\sqrt{cd} \\cdot \\sqrt{da} = 16abcd.\n$$\n\n### Step 3: Bounding the Expression\nUsing the above result:\n$$\n\\frac{(a + b)(b + c)(c + d)(d + a)}{abcd} \\geq 16.\n$$\n\nApplying AM-GM to the original expression \\(A\\):\n$$\n\\frac{A}{4} \\geq \\sqrt[4]{\\left(\\frac{a+b}{c}\\right)^4 \\left(\\frac{b+c}{d}\\right)^4 \\left(\\frac{c+d}{a}\\right)^4 \\left(\\frac{d+a}{b}\\right)^4}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the right-hand side:\n$$\n\\frac{A}{4} \\geq \\frac{(a + b)(b + c)(c + d)(d + a)}{abcd} \\geq 16.\n$$\n\nThus:\n$$\nA \\geq 4 \\times 16 = 64.\n$$\n\n### Step 4: Equality Condition\nEquality in AM-GM occurs when all terms are equal. For \\(A = 64\\), this requires:\n- \\(a = b = c = d\\).\n\n### Conclusion\nThe minimum value of \\(A\\) is achieved when all variables are equal, resulting in:\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe minimum value of \\(A\\) is \\(\\boxed{64}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "1. We need to find the minimum value of the expression \n   \\[\n   A = \\left(\\frac{a+b}{c}\\right)^4 + \\left(\\frac{b+c}{d}\\right)^4 + \\left(\\frac{c+d}{a}\\right)^4 + \\left(\\frac{d+a}{b}\\right)^4,\n   \\]\n   where \\( a, b, c, d \\) are positive numbers.\n\n2. To find this minimum value, we will use the Arithmetic Mean-Geometric Mean Inequality (AM-GM Inequality), which states that for non-negative real numbers \\( x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\),\n   \\[\n   \\frac{x_1 + x_2 + \\cdots + x_n}{n} \\geq \\sqrt[n]{x_1 x_2 \\cdots x_n}\n   \\]\n   with equality if and only if \\( x_1 = x_2 = \\cdots = x_n \\).\n\n3. Applying the AM-GM inequality to each fraction term:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{a+b}{2\\sqrt{ab}} \\geq 1,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{b+c}{2\\sqrt{bc}} \\geq 1,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{c+d}{2\\sqrt{cd}} \\geq 1,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{d+a}{2\\sqrt{da}} \\geq 1.\n   \\]\n\n4. Therefore, \n   \\[\n   \\left(\\frac{a+b}{c}\\right)^4 \\geq \\left(2 \\