ent medicines.\n\nThen x + y + z = 68.\n\nEach prescription with 1 potent medicine contributes C(1,1)*C(4,2) = 1*6 = 6 triples with 1 potent and 2 non-potent.\n\nWait, no. Wait, in a prescription with 1 potent (P) and 4 non-potent (Q), the number of triples with exactly 1 P is C(1,1)*C(4,2) = 6, and the number of triples with 0 P is C(4,3) = 4. But since each prescription must have at least 1 P, the triples with 0 P are allowed as long as the prescription has at least 1 P.\n\nSimilarly, for a prescription with 2 P and 3 Q: the number of triples with 2 P is C(2,2)*C(3,1) = 1*3 = 3, triples with 1 P are C(2,1)*C(3,2) = 2*3 = 6, and triples with 0 P are C(3,3) = 1.\n\nFor a prescription with 3 P and 2 Q: triples with 3 P: C(3,3)*C(2,0)=1, triples with 2 P: C(3,2)*C(2,1)=3*2=6, triples with 1 P: C(3,1)*C(2,2)=3*1=3.\n\nSo, total triples from all prescriptions:\n\nFor x prescriptions with 1 P:\n\n- Triples with 1 P: 6x\n\n- Triples with 0 P: 4x\n\nFor y prescriptions with 2 P:\n\n- Triples with 2 P: 3y\n\n- Triples with 1 P: 6y\n\n- Triples with 0 P: 1y\n\nFor z prescriptions with 3 P:\n\n- Triples with 3 P: 1z\n\n- Triples with 2 P: 6z\n\n- Triples with 1 P: 3z\n\nAdditionally, the total number of triples with 0 P is 4x + y. But all such triples (entirely in Q) must be accounted for. However, the total number of triples entirely in Q is C(q,3). Since q = 17 - p, where p is the number of potent medicines.\n\nBut also, all triples entirely in Q must be covered by prescriptions that have at least one P. So, each triple in Q is in exactly one prescription, which has some P elements. Therefore, the total number of triples entirely in Q is equal to the total number of 0 P triples in all prescriptions, which is 4x + y. So:\n\nC(q,3) = 4x + y.\n\nSimilarly, the total number of triples with 1 P is 6x + 6y + 3z.\n\nThe total number of triples with 1 P is also equal to C(p,1)*C(q,2)*1, since each such triple has 1 from P and 2 from Q, and each is in exactly one prescription. So:\n