|, which equals |P1T| - |P2T| (since moving towards T, the length from P1 to P2 along the tangent is |P1T| - |P2T|). Therefore, the signed length is (|P1T| - |P2T|), which is equal to -( |P2T| - |P1T| ). So the power change is (|P2T| - |P1T|)(|P2T| + |P1T|) = - (signed length) * (|P2T| + |P1T|). Therefore, if we denote the signed length as S_i = |P_{i-1} T_i| - |P_i T_i|, then the power change over that segment is -S_i * (|P_{i-1} T_i| + |P_i T_i|). But the total power change around the loop is zero, so \u03a3 ( -S_i * (|P_{i-1} T_i| + |P_i T_i|) ) = 0. However, this doesn't directly give \u03a3 S_i = 0, unless we can show that each term (|P_{i-1} T_i| + |P_i T_i|) is constant or relates to S_i in a way that allows factoring out.\n\nThis seems complicated. Maybe another approach.\n\nSuppose we parameterize each tangent segment by its point of tangency T. Let\u2019s consider that each tangent segment corresponds to moving along the tangent at T from point P to point Q. The signed length is +|PQ| if moving towards T, -|PQ| if moving away. Now, for the entire closed path, the total displacement is zero (since it's a closed polygon). However, displacement is a vector, while the sum we need is scalar. But maybe consider projecting the displacement onto some direction.\n\nAlternatively, think of the entire journey as starting at A, moving along various tangents, and returning to A. For each tangent segment, the movement along the tangent can be decomposed into a part towards the circle and a part away. But since it's a closed path, the total towards movements must balance the total away movements.\n\nWait, that\u2019s an intuitive way to put it. If you end up where you started, the total distance you moved towards the circle must equal the total distance you moved away. Therefore, the sum of the positive segments (approaching) equals the sum of the negative segments (receding), so their difference (the total signed sum) is zero.\n\nBut to make this rigorous, we need a mathematical foundation. Maybe consider a potential function that measures the distance from the circle and integrate its derivative along the path.\n\nLet\u2019s define a function f(P) = distance from P to the circle. For a point P outside the circle, f(P) = |OP| - r. For a point inside, it would be negative, but since all tangent segments are outside or on the circle (as tangents from inside would not be possible unless the point is on the circle, but tangents from inside points don't exist). Wait, actually, if A is inside the circle, then there are no tangents from A to the circle. So the polygon must lie entirely outside or on the circle. Therefore, f(P) is non-negative.\n\nThe derivative of f along the path would be the rate of change of the distance to the circle as we move along the tangent. For a segment where we are approaching the circle, the derivative is negative (since distance is decreasing), and the integral over that segment would be -length (since df/dt = -speed if moving towards the circle at unit speed). Similarly, moving away, the integral would be +length. Wait, but integrating the derivative around the closed loop should give zero, because the integral of df over a closed loop is the total change in f, which is zero.\n\nBut the integral of the derivative of f around the loop is equal to the sum of the integrals over each segment. Each integral is the signed length: negative when approaching (since derivative is negative) and positive when receding. Therefore, the total integral is zero, which implies the sum of the signed lengths is zero.\n\nYes, this seems like the right approach. Let me formalize this.\n\nDefine f(P) = distance from P to the circle, which is |OP| - r for points outside the circle. The differential of f along a direction v is given by the directional derivative D_v f(P) = (v \u00b7 OP) / |OP|, since the gradient of f is the unit vector in the direction of OP. However, when moving along a tangent to the circle, the direction of motion is perpendicular to OP at the point of tangency T. Wait, no. If we are on a tangent line at T, then the direction of the tangent is perpendicular to OT. Therefore, if moving along the tangent line, the directional derivative of f in the tangent direction would depend on the position along the tangent.\n\nWait, maybe this is getting too involved. Alternatively, consider that when moving along a tangent line towards the point of tangency T, the distance to the circle decreases at a constant rate, since the path is straight. Similarly, moving away increases the distance at a constant rate.\n\nTherefore, for each tangent segment, if we parameterize the movement from point P to point Q along the tangent, the total change in f is f(Q) - f(P). But since the path is closed, the total change in f around the loop is zero. Therefore, \u03a3 (f(Q_i) - f(P_i)) = 0, where the sum is over all segments.\n\nBut for each segment, the change in f is equal to the integral of the derivative of f along the segment. If moving towards the circle, f decreases by the length of the segment; moving away, f increases by the length. Therefore, the integral over the segment is equal to -length (approach) or +length (recede). Therefore, \u03a3 (signed lengths) = \u03a3 ( - (f(Q_i) - f(P_i)) ). But \u03a3 (f(Q_i) - f(P_i)) = 0, so \u03a3 (signed lengths) = 0.\n\nWait, let me check this again. Suppose we move from P to Q along a tangent, approaching the circle. Then f(P) > f(Q), and the change f(Q) - f(P) is negative. The signed length is +|PQ| (approaching), so we have |PQ| = f(P) - f(Q). Similarly, if receding, |PQ| = f(Q) - f(P), but signed as -|PQ|, so it's -(f(Q) - f(P)) = f(P) - f(Q). Therefore, for each segment, whether approaching or receding, the signed length is f(P) - f(Q). Then summing over all segments, \u03a3 (f(P_i) - f(Q_i)) = \u03a3 f(P_i) - \u03a3 f(Q_i). But in a closed loop, each Q_i is the P_{i+1} of the next segment. Therefore, \u03a3 f(P_i) - \u03a3 f(Q_i) = \u03a3 f(P_i) - \u03a3 f(P_{i+1}) = f(P_1) - f(P_{n+1})). Since the path is closed, P_{n+1} = P_1, so the total sum is f(P_1) - f(P_1) = 0. Hence, \u03a3 signed lengths = 0.\n\nYes, this makes sense! So each signed segment's length is equal to the difference in the distance function from the start and end points of the segment. When you sum these differences over a closed loop, they telescope to zero. Therefore, the sum of the signed lengths is zero.\n\nTherefore, the key idea is that the signed length of each segment corresponds to the change in the distance to the circle, and the total change around a closed loop is zero.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that the sum of the signed segments is zero, consider the distance function from points on the polygonal path to the circle. Let \\( f(P) \\) denote the distance from a point \\( P \\) to the circle. For each tangent segment traversed:\n\n- **Approaching the circle (positive segment):** The distance \\( f(P) \\) decreases by the length of the segment.\n- **Receding from the circle (negative segment):** The distance \\( f(P) \\) increases by the length of the segment.\n\nThe signed length of a segment is thus \\( f(\\text{start}) - f(\\text{end}) \\). Summing over all segments in the closed loop:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\text{segments}} \\left( f(P_i) - f(P_{i+1}) \\right) = f(P_1) - f(P_{n+1}),\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( P_1 = P_{n+1} = A \\) since the path is closed. This telescopes to \\( f(A) - f(A) = 0 \\). Therefore, the sum of the signed segments equals zero.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{0}", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Understanding the Given Problem**:\n\n   We have a circle and a point \\( A \\) on the plane. Starting from point \\( A \\), we traverse along a closed broken line (a sequence of straight line segments) where each segment is a tangent to the circle. Our goal is to return back to point \\( A \\). We need to show that the sum of the distances traveled towards the circle (positive sign) and away from the circle (negative sign) equals zero.\n\n2. **Tangents and Their Properties**:\n\n   A tangent to the circle from point \\( A \\) either touches the circle in one spot. For each tangent segment, we can assign it a direction and length. \n\n3. **Visualizing the Path**:\n   \n   Consider the setup: each tangent line will touch the circle at exactly one point and then extend from that point. When traveling on such a path, the net effect (towards or away from the circle) should be considered.\n\n4. **Segments and Direction Analysis**:\n   \n   - Let's denote the tangent segments by \\( T_1, T_2, \\ldots, T_n \\) where from \\( T_i \\) we move to \\( T_{i+1} \\), and so on, until \\( T_n \\) returns us to the initial point \\( T_1 = A \\).\n   - Each segment \\( T_i \\) can be analyzed based on whether it is moving closer to the circle (positive direction) or farther from the circle (negative direction).\n\n5. **Sum of Signed Distances**:\n   \n   - For each segment, we specifically denote the distance in the positive direction if it approaches the circle and in the negative direction if it moves away.\n   - Because we are traversing a closed path, every move away from the circle must be balanced by a move towards the circle to return to the starting point \\( A \\).\n\n6. **Geometric Interpretation**:\n   \n   - The heart of the proof lies in the geometric properties of tangents. Tangents from a single external point to a circle have equal lengths.\n   - Since the path is closed, the net distance vertically or horizontally in the overall plane will also balance out to zero, not just in the circular context but any repeating linear sections will aggregate to zero.\n\n7. **Final Conclusion**:\n   \n   - Putting it all together, each segment towards the circle essentially cancels out with a corresponding segment moving away when considering all tangents combined in the closed path.\n\nThus, the sum of the signed distances, considering each towards (positive) and away (negative) path, resul