en subtract b to go to B. In B, we subtract b until we can't, then add a to go back to A.\n\nWait, let's take the earlier example where a = 1, b = 1, A = {2}, B = {1}. The function f would be:\n\nFor 2 \u2208 A: 2 + 1 = 3 \u2209 A, so f(2) = 2 - 1 = 1 \u2208 B.\n\nFor 1 \u2208 B: 1 - 1 = 0 \u2209 B, so f(1) = 1 + 1 = 2 \u2208 A.\n\nSo the cycle is 2 \u2192 1 \u2192 2 \u2192 ...\n\nThis cycle has two elements, one in A and one in B. The number of additions and subtractions: from A to B, we subtract 1 (b=1), from B to A, we add 1 (a=1). So total displacement: -1 +1 = 0. The number of additions is 1, subtractions is 1. So m/n = 1/1 = b/a = 1/1. So ratio holds.\n\nIn this case, |A| = 1, |B| = 1. So a|A| = 1*1 = 1, b|B| = 1*1 = 1. Equality holds.\n\nAnother example: a = 2, b = 1, A = {4}, B = {2, 3}.\n\nWait, let's check the cycles here.\n\nFor 4 \u2208 A: 4 + 2 = 6 \u2209 A, so f(4) = 4 - 1 = 3 \u2208 B.\n\nFor 3 \u2208 B: 3 - 1 = 2 \u2208 B.\n\nFor 2 \u2208 B: 2 - 1 = 1 \u2209 B, so f(2) = 2 + 2 = 4 \u2208 A.\n\nSo the cycle is 4 \u2192 3 \u2192 2 \u2192 4 \u2192 ...\n\nHere, starting from 4 (A), subtract 1 to 3 (B), subtract 1 to 2 (B), add 2 to 4 (A). So in this cycle, there's one addition (a=2) and two subtractions (b=1). The total displacement: -1 -1 +2 = 0. So m = 1 addition, n = 2 subtractions. Then m/n = 1/2 = b/a =1/2. So the ratio holds. The number of elements in A in this cycle is 1 (element 4), and in B is 2 (elements 3 and 2). So |A| =1, |B|=2. Then a|A| =2*1=2, b|B| =1*2=2. So equality holds.\n\nTherefore, in each cycle, the number of elements in A and B are proportional to b and a, respectively. So if all cycles have this property, then the total |A| and |B| would satisfy a|A| = b|B|.\n\nTherefore, the key is to show that each cycle contributes b elements to A and a elements to B, or some multiple thereof, such that the total counts maintain the ratio.\n\nAlternatively, since each cycle has m additions of a and n subtractions of b with m/n = b/a, the numb