(-\u221a3,1)\n\nNow, find DC and BE.\n\nPoint D is (1, -\u221a3), C is (0,2). So DC is the line from (1, -\u221a3) to (0,2).\n\nBE is from B(2,0) to E(-\u221a3,1).\n\nFind intersection F of DC and BE.\n\nFirst, parametrize DC:\n\nDC: from (1, -\u221a3) to (0,2). Let parameter be s \u2208 [0,1].\n\nx = 1 - s\n\ny = -\u221a3 + (2 + \u221a3)s\n\nBE: from (2,0) to (-\u221a3,1). Let parameter be t \u2208 [0,1].\n\nx = 2 - (2 + \u221a3)t\n\ny = 0 + t\n\nSet equations equal:\n\n1 - s = 2 - (2 + \u221a3)t\n\n-\u221a3 + (2 + \u221a3)s = t\n\nFrom first equation:\n\ns = (2 + \u221a3)t -1\n\nSubstitute into second equation:\n\n-\u221a3 + (2 + \u221a3)( (2 + \u221a3)t -1 ) = t\n\nExpand:\n\n-\u221a3 + (2 + \u221a3)(2 + \u221a3)t - (2 + \u221a3) = t\n\nCompute (2 + \u221a3)(2 + \u221a3) = 4 + 4\u221a3 + 3 = 7 + 4\u221a3\n\nSo:\n\n-\u221a3 + (7 + 4\u221a3)t - (2 + \u221a3) = t\n\nCombine constants:\n\n-\u221a3 -2 - \u221a3 = -2 - 2\u221a3\n\nSo:\n\n-2 - 2\u221a3 + (7 + 4\u221a3)t = t\n\nBring terms with t to left:\n\n(7 + 4\u221a3 -1)t = 2 + 2\u221a3\n\n(6 + 4\u221a3)t = 2 + 2\u221a3\n\nDivide both sides by 2:\n\n(3 + 2\u221a3)t = 1 + \u221a3\n\nSolve for t:\n\nt = (1 + \u221a3)/(3 + 2\u221a3)\n\nRationalize denominator:\n\nMultiply numerator and denominator by (3 - 2\u221a3):\n\nt = (1 + \u221a3)(3 - 2\u221a3) / [ (3 + 2\u221a3)(3 - 2\u221a3) ]\n\nDenominator: 9 - (2\u221a3)^2 = 9 - 12 = -3\n\nNumerator: 1*3 + 1*(-2\u221a3) + \u221a3*3 + \u221a3*(-2\u221a3)\n\n= 3 - 2\u221a3 + 3\u221a3 - 6\n\n= (3 -6) + (-2\u221a3 + 3\u221a3)\n\n= -3 + \u221a3\n\nTherefore, t = (-3 + \u221a3)/(-3) = (3 - \u221a3)/3 = 1 - (\u221a3)/3\n\nTherefore, t = 1 - \u221a3/3 \u2248 1 - 0.577 \u2248 0.423\n\nThen, coordinates of F:\n\nx = 2 - (2 + \u221a3)t = 2 - (2 + \u221a3)(1 - \u221a3/3)\n\nCompute this:\n\n(2 + \u221a3)(1 - \u221a3/3) = 2*(1) + 2*(-\u221a3/3) + \u221a3*1 + \u221a3*(-\u221a3/3)\n\n= 2 - (2\u221a3)/3 + \u221a3 - (3)/3\n\n= 2 - (2\u221a3)/3 + \u221a3 -1\n\n= (2 -1) + [ -2\u221a3/3 + 3\u221a3/3 ]\n\n= 1 + (\u221a3)/3\n\nTherefore, x = 2 - [1 + \u221a3/3] = 1 - \u221a3/3\n\ny = t = 1 - \u221a3/3\n\nSo F is at (1 - \u221a3/3, 1 - \u221a3/3)\n\nNow, compute angle AFB.\n\nPoints:\n\nA(0,0), F(1 - \u221a3/3, 1 - \u221a3/3), B(2,0)\n\nVectors:\n\nFA = A - F = (-1 + \u221a3/3, -1 + \u221a3/3)\n\nFB = B - F = (2 - (1 - \u221a3/3), 0 - (1 - \u221a3/3)) = (1 + \u221a3/3, -1 + \u221a3/3)\n\nCompute the angle between FA and FB.\n\nDot product:\n\nFA \u22c5 FB = [(-1 + \u221a3/3)(1 + \u221a3/3)] + [(-1 + \u221a3/3)(-1 + \u221a3/3)]\n\nFirst term:\n\n(-1)(1) + (-1)(\u221a3/3) + (\u221a3/3)(1) + (\u221a3/3)(\u221a3/3)\n\n= -1 - \u221a3/3 + \u221a3/3 + (3/9)\n\n= -1 + 1/3\n\n= -2/3\n\nSecond term:\n\n(-1 + \u221a3/3)^2 = 1 - 2\u221a3/3 + (3)/9 = 1 - 2\u221a3/3 + 1/3 = 4/3 - 2\u221a3/3\n\nSo total FA \u22c5 FB = -2/3 + (4/3 - 2\u221a3/3) = ( -2/3 + 4/3 ) - 2\u221a3/3 = 2/3 - 2\u221a3/3\n\nBut wait, this doesn't seem right. Wait, actually, the vectors FA and FB are both two-dimensional, and the dot product is calculated as (FA_x * FB_x + FA_y * FB_y).\n\nLet me recompute:\n\nFA = (-1 + \u221a3/3, -1 + \u221a3/3)\n\nFB = (1 + \u221a3/3, -1 + \u221a3/3)\n\nDot product = (-1 + \u221a3/3)(1 + \u221a3/3) + (-1 + \u221a3/3)(-1 + \u221a3/3)\n\nFirst term:\n\nMultiply (-1 + \u221a3/3)(1 + \u221a3/3):\n\n= (-1)(1) + (-1)(\u221a3/3) + (\u221a3/3)(1) + (\u221a3/3)(\u221a3/3)\n\n= -1 - \u221a3/3 + \u221a3/3 + 1/3\n\n= -1 + 1/3\n\n= -2/3\n\nSecond term:\n\n(-1 + \u221a3/3)(-1 + \u221a3/3) = (-1)^2 + (\u221a3/3)^2 - 2*(-1)*(\u221a3/3)\n\n= 1 + 1/3 + 2\u221a3/3\n\n= 4/3 + 2\u221a3/3\n\nTotal dot product: -2/3 + 4/3 + 2\u221a3/3 = (2/3) + (2\u221a3/3)\n\nMagnitude of FA:\n\n|FA| = sqrt[ (-1 + \u221a3/3)^2 + (-1 + \u221a3/3)^2 ] = sqrt[2*(-1 + \u221a3/3)^2 ] = sqrt[2*(1 - 2\u221a3/3 + 1/3)] = sqrt[2*(4/3 - 2\u221a3/3)] = sqrt[8/3 - 4\u221a3/3]\n\nSimilarly, |FB|:\n\nsqrt[ (1 + \u221a3/3)^2 + (-1 + \u221a3/3)^2 ]\n\nFirst term: 1 + 2\u221a3/3 + 1/3\n\nSecond term: 1 - 2\u221a3/3 + 1/3\n\nTotal: (1 + 1/3 + 2\u221a3/3) + (1 + 1/3 - 2\u221a3/3) = (4/3 + 2\u221a3/3) + (4/3 - 2\u221a3/3) = 8/3\n\nSo |FB| = sqrt(8/3) = 2*sqrt(6)/3 \u2248 1.632\n\nSimilarly, |FA| = sqrt(8/3 - 4\u221a3/3) \u2248 ?\n\nWait, this is getting complicated. Alternatively, compute the cosine of the angle:\n\ncos\u03b8 = (FA \u22c5 FB) / (|FA| |FB|)\n\nWe have FA \u22c5 FB = 2/3 + 2\u221a3/3\n\n|FA| = sqrt(2*( (-1 + \u221a3/3)^2 )) = sqrt(2*(1 - 2\u221a3/3 + 1/3)) = sqrt(2*(4/3 - 2\u221a3/3)) = sqrt(8/3 - 4\u221a3/3)\n\n|FB| = sqrt(8/3)\n\nSo,\n\ncos\u03b8 = (2/3 + 2\u221a3/3) / ( sqrt(8/3 - 4\u221a3/3) * sqrt(8/3) )\n\nThis seems messy. Maybe compute numerically:\n\n\u221a3 \u2248 1.732\n\nCompute numerator: 2/3 + 2*1.732/3 \u2248 0.6667 + 1.1547 \u2248 1.8214\n\nDenominator:\n\nFirst sqrt: 8/3 - 4*1.732/3 \u2248 2.6667 - 2.309 \u2248 0.3577\n\nsqrt(0.3577) \u2248 0.598\n\nSecond sqrt: sqrt(8/3) \u2248 1.632\n\nMultiply: 0.598 * 1.632 \u2248 0.977\n\nSo cos\u03b8 \u2248 1.8214 / 0.977 \u2248 1.864\n\nBut cosine can't be more than 1. This indicates an error in calculation.\n\nWait, my FA \u22c5 FB calculation must be wrong. Let's recheck.\n\nFA = (-1 + \u221a3/3, -1 + \u221a3/3)\n\nFB = (1 + \u221a3/3, -1 + \u221a3/3)\n\nDot product = (-1 + \u221a3/3)(1 + \u221a3/3) + (-1 + \u221a3/3)(-1 + \u221a3/3)\n\nFirst term:\n\n(-1)(1) + (-1)(\u221a3/3) + (\u221a3/3)(1) + (\u221a3/3)(\u221a3/3)\n\n= -1 - \u221a3/3 + \u221a3/3 + (3/9)\n\n= -1 + 1/3\n\n= -2/3\n\nSecond term:\n\n(-1 + \u221a3/3)(-1 + \u221a3/3) = (-1)^2 + (\u221a3/3)^2 + 2*(1)*(\u221a3/3)\n\nWait, no, expanding (-1 + a)(-1 + a) = 1 - 2a + a\u00b2. Here, a = \u221a3/3\n\nSo it's 1 - 2*(\u221a3/3) + (\u221a3/3)^2 = 1 - 2\u221a3/3 + 3/9 = 1 - 2\u221a3/3 + 1/3 = 4/3 - 2\u221a3/3\n\nTherefore, total dot product = -2/3 + 4/3 - 2\u221a3/3 = (2/3) - (2\u221a3/3)\n\nAh, I missed the negative sign in the second term. So FA \u22c5 FB = (-2/3) + (4/3 - 2\u221a3/3) = (2/3) - (2\u221a3/3)\n\nTherefore, cos\u03b8 = (2/3 - 2\u221a3/3) / (|FA| |FB| )\n\nCompute |FA|:\n\nsqrt[ (-1 + \u221a3/3)^2 + (-1 + \u221a3/3)^2 ] = sqrt[ 2*(1 - 2\u221a3/3 + 1/3) ] = sqrt[ 2*(4/3 - 2\u221a3/3) ] = sqrt(8/3 - 4\u221a3/3)\n\nSimilarly, |FB| = sqrt[ (1 + \u221a3/3)^2 + (-1 + \u221a3/3)^2 ] = sqrt[ (1 + 2\u221a3/3 + 1/3) + (1 - 2\u221a3/3 + 1/3) ] = sqrt[ (4/3 + 2\u221a3/3) + (4/3 - 2\u221a3/3) ] = sqrt[8/3] = 2*sqrt(6)/3 \u2248 1.632\n\nCompute denominator:\n\nsqrt(8/3 - 4\u221a3/3) * 2*sqrt(6)/3\n\nCalculate 8/3 \u2248 2.6667, 4\u221a3/3 \u2248 4*1.732/3 \u2248 2.309. So 8/3 - 4\u221a3/3 \u2248 0.3577. sqrt(0.3577) \u2248 0.598.\n\nThen denominator \u2248 0.598 * 1.632 \u2248 0.977\n\nNumerator: 2/3 - 2\u221a3/3 \u2248 0.6667 - 1.1547 \u2248 -0.488\n\nThus, cos\u03b8 \u2248 -0.488 / 0.977 \u2248 -0.5\n\nTherefore, \u03b8 \u2248 120 degrees.\n\nSo even in this right-angled triangle case, angle AFB is 120 degrees. Hence, it's consistent with the previous result. Therefore, regardless of the triangle, angle AFB is 120 degrees.\n\nTherefore, the answer to part b is 120 degrees, or 2\u03c0/3 radians.\n\n**Final Answer**\na) \\boxed{DC = BE}\n\nb) \\boxed{120^\\circ}", "deepseek_solution": "Given any triangle \\(ABC\\), we need to verify that \\(DC = BE\\) and find the angle \\(\\angle AFB\\) where \\(F\\) is the intersection point of \\(DC\\) and \\(BE\\).\n\n### Part a: Verify that \\(DC = BE\\)\n\n1. **Constructing Equilateral Triangles**:\n   - Draw equilateral triangles \\(ABD\\) and \\(ACE\\) externally on \\(AB\\) and \\(AC\\) respectively.\n   - Rotate vector \\(AB\\) by \\(-60^\\circ\\) (clockwise) to get vector \\(AD\\).\n   - Rotate vector \\(AC\\) by \\(60^\\circ\\) (counterclockwise) to get vector \\(AE\\).\n\n2. **Using Rotations**:\n   - The rotation of \\(AB\\) by \\(-60^\\circ\\) results in \\(AD\\).\n   - The rotation of \\(AC\\) by \\(60^\\circ\\) results in \\(AE\\).\n\n3. **Vector Analysis**:\n   - Vector \\(DC = \\vec{c} - \\vec{AD}\\).\n   - Vector \\(BE = \\vec{AE} - \\vec{AB}\\).\n   - By the properties of rotations, the magnitudes of \\(DC\\) and \\(BE\\) are equal because they are related by a rotation which preserves distances.\n\n4. **Conclusion**:\n   - Since the rotations preserve distances, \\(DC = BE\\).\n\n### Part b: Find the angle \\(\\angle AFB\\)\n\n1. **Specific Coordinate System**:\n   - Consider \\(ABC\\) as an equilateral triangle with \\(A(0,0)\\), \\(B(2,0)\\), and \\(C(1, \\sqrt{3})\\).\n   - Construct \\(D\\) and \\(E\\) by rotating \\(AB\\) and \\(AC\\) appropriately.\n\n2. **Intersection Point \\(F\\)**:\n   - Calculate the coordinates of \\(D\\) and \\(E\\).\n   - Determine the intersection \\(F\\) of lines \\(DC\\) and \\(BE\\).\n\n3. **Angle Calculation**:\n   - Using coordinates, vectors, and dot product, compute the angle \\(\\angle AFB\\).\n   - For any triangle \\(ABC\\), the angle \\(\\angle AFB\\) is found to be \\(120^\\circ\\) due to the rotational symmetry and properties of equilateral triangles.\n\n### Final Answers\n\na) \\(\\boxed{DC = BE}\\)\n\nb) \\(\\boxed{120^\\circ}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\nLet's analyze the given problem step-by-step. We have a triangle \\(ABC\\) and two exterior equilateral triangles \\(ABD\\) and \\(ACE\\). We need to prove two parts: first, that line segments \\(DC\\) and \\(BE\\) are equal, and second, to find the angle \\(\\angle AFB\\).\n\n**a) Verifying that \\(DC = BE\\)**\n\n1. **Lengths from equilateral triangles:**\n    - Since \\(ABD\\) is an equilateral triangle, all its sides are equal: \\(AD = AB\\).\n    - Similarly, for \\(ACE\\), all its sides are equal: \\(AE = AC\\).\n\n2. **Angles at vertex \\(A:**\n    - The angle \\(\\angle DAC = \\angle BAE\\) consists of the internal angle \\(\\angle BAC\\) of triangle \\(ABC\\) plus \\(60^\\circ\\) from the equilateral triangles.\n    \\[\n    \\angle DAC = \\angle BAC + 60^\\circ\n    \\]\n    - Similarly,\n    \\[\n    \\angle BAE = \\angle BAC + 60^\\circ\n    \\]\n\n3. **Congruence of triangles:**\n    - Consider triangles \\(ADC\\) and \\(ABE\\). We have:\n        - \\(AD = AB\\) from \\(ABD\\),\n        - \\(AE = AC\\) from \\(ACE\\),\n        - \\(\\angle DAC = \\angle BAE\\) as established.\n    - By the Side-Angle-Side (SAS) congruence postulate, these triangles are congruent:\n    \\[\n    \\triangle ADC \\cong \\triangle ABE\n    \\]\n    - Therefore, corresponding sides \\(DC\\) and \\(BE\\) are equal:\n    \\[\n    DC = BE\n    \\]\n\n**b) Finding the angle \\(\\angle AFB\\)**\n\n1. **Using Point \\(F\\) as the intersection of \\(DC\\) and \\(BE\\):**\n    - From the previous congruence,\n    \\[\n    \\angle ADF = \\angle ADB \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\angle ABF = \\angle ABE\n    \\]\n    \n2. **Considering the circumcircle:**\n    - If \\(C\\) lies on the circumcircle passing through points \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(D\\), and \\(X\\) is the intersection of line \\(DC\\) with this circle,\n    \\[\n    \\angle ADC = \\angle ADX \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\angle ABE = \\angle ABX\n    \\]\n    - Since \\(X\\) is also on line \\(BE\\), it necessarily coincides with \\(F\\).\n\n3. **Calculating angle \\(\\angle AFB\\):**\n    - Knowing \\(F\\) lies on the circumcircle,\n    \\[\n    \\angle AFB = 180^\\circ - \\angle ADB\n    \\]\n    - Since \\(\\triangle ABD\\) is equilateral, \\(\\angle ADB = 60^\\circ\\),\n    \\[\n    \\angle AFB = 180^\\circ - 60^\\circ = 120^\\circ\n    \\]\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nThe final results are:\n1. \\(DC = BE\\)\n2. \\(\\angle AFB = 120^\\circ\\)\n\nHence,\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle AFB = 120^\\circ}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "In each cell of a table of size \\((n-2) \\times n\\) (where \\(n > 2\\)) an integer from 1 to \\(n\\) is written, such that all numbers in each row are different and all numbers in each column are different. Prove that this table can be extended to an \\(n \\times n\\) square by filling in each new cell with some integer from 1 to \\(n\\) in such a way that all numbers in each row and in each column remain different.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that a table of size (n-2) \u00d7 n, where each row and column has distinct integers from 1 to n, can be extended to an n \u00d7 n Latin square. Hmm, let me start by recalling what a Latin square is. A Latin square is an n \u00d7 n grid where each number from 1 to n appears exactly once in each row and each column. The given table is already a partial Latin square, but missing the last two rows. My goal is to show that we can add those two rows (and possibly adjust some existing entries if needed?) to complete it into a full Latin square. Wait, but the problem states that the existing cells are already filled with numbers such that each row and column has distinct numbers. So the existing part is a proper partial Latin square, and we need to extend it by adding two more rows. But wait, the problem says \"filling in each new cell with some integer from 1 to n\", so I guess the existing entries stay as they are, and we just need to add two more rows at the bottom, each of length n, such that in each new row, all numbers are distinct, and also in each column, the numbers are distinct when considering the existing entries plus the new ones. Wait, but the existing columns already have (n-2) entries, each distinct. So each column is missing two numbers. So perhaps each column needs to have those two missing numbers filled in the two new rows. But the two new rows also need to have distinct numbers in each row. So the problem reduces to arranging the missing numbers for each column into the two new rows such that each row is a permutation of 1 to n. This seems related to completing a partial Latin square. There's a theorem called Hall's Marriage Theorem that might be applicable here. Let me recall: Hall's theorem states that a family of sets has a system of distinct representatives (SDR) if and only if for every subset of the family, the union of the sets in the