re identified. Each pair consists of distinct primes, ensuring their GCD is 1 (automatically coprime). This yields **30 valid pairs**:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n11 + 409, &\\quad 19 + 401, \\quad 23 + 397, \\quad 31 + 389, \\quad 37 + 383, \\\\\n41 + 379, &\\quad 47 + 373, \\quad 53 + 367, \\quad 61 + 359, \\quad 67 + 353, \\\\\n71 + 349, &\\quad 73 + 347, \\quad 83 + 337, \\quad 89 + 331, \\quad 103 + 317, \\\\\n107 + 313, &\\quad 109 + 311, \\quad 113 + 307, \\quad 127 + 293, \\quad 137 + 283, \\\\\n139 + 281, &\\quad 149 + 271, \\quad 151 + 269, \\quad 157 + 263, \\quad 163 + 257, \\\\\n179 + 241, &\\quad 181 + 239, \\quad 191 + 229, \\quad 193 + 227, \\quad 197 + 223.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\n**Answer**: \\(\\boxed{30}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\n\nThe problem is to find the number of ways to express the number 420 as the sum of two natural numbers such that:\n1. The two numbers are coprime with each other.\n2. Both numbers are prime.\n\n#### Step-by-step Solution:\n\n### I. Finding Pairs that are Relatively Prime\n\nWe want to find pairs \\((u, v)\\) such that \\( u + v = 420 \\) and \\( u \\) and \\( v \\) are coprime (i.e., \\(\\gcd(u, v) = 1\\)). By symmetry, we can assume \\( u \\leq v \\), thus \\( u \\leq 210 \\).\n\nSince \\( u \\) and \\( v \\) are coprime, both must be coprime with 420. To find such \\( u \\), we need to count the numbers up to 210 that are coprime with the prime factors of 420 (which are 2, 3, 5, and 7).\n\n#### Sieve Method for Counting Numbers up to 210 that are Coprime with 420\n\n1. The prime factorization of 420 is \\( 2^2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 7 \\).\n2. We need to exclude multiples of 2, 3, 5, and 7 up to 210 using the principle of inclusion-exclusion.\n\nLet's first count the multiples and apply inclusion-exclusion principle:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Multiples of } 2 & = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{210}{2} \\right\\rfloor = 105, \\\\\n\\text{Multiples of } 3 & = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{210}{3} \\right\\rfloor = 70, \\\\\n