l (and not intersect). Since we need each line to have 3 points, parallel lines would need their own separate points, which might not be efficient. So, perhaps making all lines intersect at various points would allow more sharing.\n\nWait, but if lines are concurrent (all passing through a single point), then that point is on all lines. However, each line still needs two more points. So, if all 6 lines pass through one common point, then each line needs two more unique points. That would give a total of 1 (common point) + 6*2 = 13 points. But maybe some of those two points per line can also be shared among other lines.\n\nWait, but if the lines are all passing through the common point, then the other two points on each line could be arranged such that some lines share those other points. But how?\n\nSuppose we have 6 lines passing through a common point P. Each line needs two more points. Let's name the lines L1, L2, L3, L4, L5, L6. Each Li has point P and two others. If the other points on each line are unique, then we have 12 points. But maybe some lines can share their other points.\n\nBut if two lines share another point, say Q, then Q is on both lines. But since those two lines already intersect at P, they can't intersect again unless they are the same line, which they aren't. So, two distinct lines can't share two points unless they are the same line. Therefore, the two lines passing through P can share at most one other point? Wait, no. If two lines intersect at P, they can't intersect again. So, if two lines share another point Q, then they would coincide, which is impossible. So, each pair of lines through P can only intersect at P. Therefore, the other points on each line must be unique to that line. Hence, if all 6 lines pass through P, then each line has two unique points, leading to 1 + 6*2 = 13 points. But maybe some of these lines can share points with other lines not passing through P. Wait, but all lines pass through P. So, their other points can't 