owing us to reduce the total number of points.\n\nLet me try to create a configuration where some points are shared by three lines.\n\nSuppose we have a configuration where three lines meet at a single point P. Then, each of those three lines needs two more points. Let's say lines L1, L2, L3 meet at P. Each of these lines needs two more points. Let's name these points as follows: L1 has P, Q1, R1; L2 has P, Q2, R2; L3 has P, Q3, R3. So far, we have 1 (P) + 6 (Q1, Q2, Q3, R1, R2, R3) = 7 points. Now, we have three more lines, L4, L5, L6. Each of these needs three points. We can try to reuse existing points.\n\nSuppose L4 is a line that goes through Q1, Q2, and Q3. If that's possible, then L4 would have three points. Similarly, L5 could go through R1, R2, R3, and L6 could go through some other combination. However, in general, three points may not be colinear unless we arrange them that way. So, if we set Q1, Q2, Q3 to be colinear, then L4 is the line through them. Similarly, R1, R2, R3 colinear for L5, and perhaps another line L6 through some other points.\n\nBut wait, in this case, arranging Q1, Q2, Q3 to be colinear requires that they lie on a line, which would be L4. Similarly for R1, R2, R3. However, in this configuration, Q1 is on L1 and L4, Q2 is on L2 and L4, Q3 is on L3 and L4. Similarly, R1 is on L1 and L5, R2 on L2 and L5, R3 on L3 and L5. Then, L6 needs three points. Maybe we can have L6 pass through P and two other points. But P is already on L1, L2, L3. If L6 also passes through P, that would be the fourth line through P, but each of the existing lines L1, L2, L3, L4, L5, L6: Wait, L4 and L5 are separate. So, if L6 passes through P, then P is on L1, L2, L3, L6. That would mean L6 has P and needs two more points. Where else can L6 go? If we have points S and T, but we don't have any other points yet. Alternatively, maybe L6 can share some existing points. For example, if L6 passes through Q1 and R1. But Q1 is on L1 and L4, R1 is on L1 and L5. If L6 passes